1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,467
On 10 June 2000, London opened the Millennium Bridge.

2
00:00:04,467 --> 00:00:06,583
It closed two days later.

3
00:00:06,583 --> 00:00:09,143
Two thousand people crossed at once.

4
00:00:09,143 --> 00:00:13,453
The deck began to sway sideways, up to 70 millimetres.

5
00:00:13,453 --> 00:00:16,405
The engineers had checked vertical loads.

6
00:00:16,405 --> 00:00:20,793
Nobody had checked what a walking crowd does to a deck sideways.

7
00:00:20,793 --> 00:00:24,712
The effect is called synchronous lateral excitation.

8
00:00:24,712 --> 00:00:32,705
When a bridge sways, people widen their stance to match it, and every corrected step pushes the deck further.

9
00:00:32,705 --> 00:00:39,784
The fix took two years and 5 million pounds: 91 dampers bolted underneath the deck.

10
00:00:39,784 --> 00:00:43,546
Design sway allowed: 5 millimetres.

11
00:00:43,546 --> 00:00:47,700
Sway measured on opening day: 70 millimetres.

12
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:49,946
The bridge was never unsafe.

13
00:00:49,946 --> 00:00:53,342
It was just honest about the physics nobody had modelled.

